# PART II — THE RESEARCH HYPOTHESIS

## 3. Research question

Test the following proposition:

A nonlinear, spatially extended, temporally persistent substrate can encode, retain, propagate and combine information deposited by multiple agents, enabling useful collective behaviour that cannot be explained merely by ordinary shared memory, direct messaging or conventional stigmergic signalling.

Separate this proposition into four independently testable claims.

### Claim A — Information encoding

An agent's deposit produces a measurable substrate-state change that represents identifiable information.

### Claim B — Persistent information storage

The information remains recoverable after the depositing agent has left and its direct activity has ceased.

### Claim C — Collective information processing

Deposits from multiple agents interact to produce a combined state that another agent can recover and use.

### Claim D — Functional advantage

The substrate produces a measurable capability or performance advantage in a collective task compared with relevant conventional communication and memory architectures.

These claims must be evaluated separately.

Do not infer Claim C from Claim B. Do not infer Claim D from Claim C.

